@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About Us";
}

<div class="container mt-4">

    <div class="fc-pagehead">
        <div class="fc-eyebrow">About</div>
        <h2>About FeedCraft</h2>
        <p class="fc-lede">
            A least-cost feed formulation tool: it finds the cheapest mix of available ingredients
            that satisfies every nutritional requirement, and reports which requirements are driving
            that cost.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="fc-prose">
        <h3>Why we built it</h3>
        <p>
            FeedCraft began with a real request. Moyaj's brother, a nutritionist at a feed company,
            was formulating rations by hand in a spreadsheet &mdash; a slow, ingredient-by-ingredient
            process in which even a small improvement in cost per kilogram matters at industrial
            batch sizes. He asked whether it could be automated properly.
        </p>
        <p>
            FeedCraft is the answer: a solver that finds the guaranteed cheapest mix meeting every
            nutritional requirement, and shows exactly which requirement is driving that cost.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* width and height are the intrinsic size of mill-1600.jpg, so the browser reserves the right
       box before the file arrives and the text below does not jump. The caption says plainly what
       the picture is: it stands in for the setting, and claiming otherwise would be the one kind of
       decoration this project cannot afford.

       srcset goes through Url.Content by hand. Razor's "~/" is expanded by UrlResolutionTagHelper,
       which only knows a fixed list of attributes -- src for an img, href for a link -- and srcset
       is not on it. Written as "~/img/..." there, the tilde ships to the browser literally and both
       candidates 404. *@
    <figure class="fc-band">
        <img src="~/img/mill-1600.jpg"
             srcset="@(Url.Content("~/img/mill-800.jpg")) 800w, @(Url.Content("~/img/mill-1600.jpg")) 1600w"
             sizes="(max-width: 1199px) 100vw, 1140px"
             width="1600" height="533" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             alt="Sacks of grain and meal stacked around a steel mixing hopper in a small feed mill, with open sacks of maize and coarse meal in the foreground." />
        <figcaption>
            Illustration, not a photograph of a real operation. Ingredients sacked, weighed and mixed
            by hand is the process the spreadsheet was tracking.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>

